<p>An AI walkthrough video is made entirely from your listing photos. Nothing is filmed, so the photos set the limit on what the film can show and how good it looks. This checklist covers what to look for before you send them, whether you run a yacht charter, a hotel or a holiday rental.</p>

<h2>Why does photo quality matter so much?</h2>
<p>AI video software takes a still photo and generates the frames around it: a slow push into a room, a pan along a deck, a turn towards a window. To do that, it has to read the detail in the photo and keep it consistent as the camera moves.</p>
<p>A sharp, well-lit photo gives it a lot to work with. Edges are clear, textures are visible and lines are easy to follow. A soft or dark photo gives it less, so the software has to guess more, and guesses are where things go wrong: edges that wobble, details that change shape, surfaces that look smeared.</p>
<p>In short, the film can be as good as the photos, but not better. Sharper photos give a sharper, calmer film.</p>

<h2>What is the checklist?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Resolution.</strong> Send the largest files you have. Images saved from a website or a booking platform are usually compressed and reduced in size. They can work, but originals work better.</li>
  <li><strong>Sharpness.</strong> Check the photo at full size. If fine details such as cushion seams, tiles or rigging look soft, the film will look soft too.</li>
  <li><strong>Light.</strong> Bright, even light works best. Very dark corners or blown-out windows leave areas with no detail for the AI to move through.</li>
  <li><strong>Straight lines.</strong> Vertical lines in walls, doors and windows should be upright. Photos taken with a tilted camera or heavy wide-angle distortion are harder to move smoothly.</li>
  <li><strong>Clean frames.</strong> No watermarks, logos, text overlays or date stamps in the image. These do not move naturally with the scene.</li>
  <li><strong>Natural colour.</strong> A light edit is fine. Heavy filters, strong HDR looks or artificial skies tend to look less natural once they move.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Wide shots or detail shots?</h2>
<p>You need both. They do different jobs in the film.</p>
<p><strong>Wide shots</strong> show the whole space: the full saloon, the deck from the bow, a hotel room from the door. They give the viewer a sense of size and layout, and they work well with slow push-ins and pans.</p>
<p><strong>Detail shots</strong> show what makes the space feel cared for: a made bed, a set table, a view through a porthole, a bathroom fitting. They work well as short moments between the wide shots.</p>
<p>A good set has a wide shot of every space and a few details. A set made only of details is hard to follow; a set made only of wide shots can feel flat.</p>

<h2>What order should the photos be in?</h2>
<p>A walkthrough works best when it follows the way a guest would move through the place. Think of it as a short tour:</p>
<ol>
  <li>An outside shot that sets the scene: the yacht underway or at anchor, or the hotel entrance and setting.</li>
  <li>The main shared spaces: deck and saloon, or lobby, restaurant and terrace.</li>
  <li>The private spaces: cabins or rooms.</li>
  <li>Bathrooms and details.</li>
  <li>A closing shot, often outside again, or a view.</li>
</ol>
<p>You do not have to put the photos in order yourself. We choose the photos, the order and the camera moves. But if you have a preferred order, tell us, and if a space has several photos from different angles, send them all so we can pick the one that moves best.</p>

<h2>What should you avoid?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Blurred photos.</strong> Motion blur or missed focus cannot be fixed in the video.</li>
  <li><strong>Dark photos.</strong> Evening shots with little light, or interiors with the lights off, give a muddy result.</li>
  <li><strong>People in the shot.</strong> The video shows spaces, not guests or crew. Faces and bodies are among the things AI moves least reliably, so we leave those photos out.</li>
  <li><strong>Watermarks and overlays.</strong> Ask your photographer for clean files.</li>
  <li><strong>Heavy filters.</strong> Strong colour grades and artificial effects tend to look odd once the camera moves.</li>
  <li><strong>Screenshots.</strong> A screenshot of a listing is much lower in quality than the photo itself.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why ask your photographer for the original files?</h2>
<p>Most listing platforms and websites compress photos so pages load quickly. That is fine for a still image on a screen, but it removes fine detail the AI uses when it creates movement. The files your photographer delivered are usually larger and sharper.</p>
<p>If you have them, send those. If not, a link to your listing is still enough to start, and we will tell you if a photo is too weak to use.</p>

<h2>What if a space was never photographed?</h2>
<p>Then it cannot be in the film. The video only shows what is in the photos. We do not add rooms, cabins, views, furniture or people that are not there, and there is no recorded sound from the scene. If a flybridge, a bathroom or a terrace is missing from your set, the simplest fix is to photograph it and send the new photo.</p>

<h2>Do you need to prepare anything else?</h2>
<p>No. You do not need any software, skills or an account. You send a link to your listing or the photos, and we do the rest. A person watches every video in full before delivery and compares it with your photos. You get the film in 16:9 and 9:16, with two rounds of revisions included. More on the steps on the <a href={pagePath(lang, 'how')}>how it works</a> page.</p>

<h2>Try it with your own photos</h2>
<p>The easiest way to see whether your photos are good enough is to try them. We make a free preview of 15 to 25 seconds, with a few shots from your own listing photos, with no obligation. <a href={pagePath(lang, 'contact')}>Send us a link to your listing</a> and we will show you what your photos can become.</p>
